  <!-- Page Header -->
  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>Control and Righteousness</h1>
    <p>The Foundational Principle Behind Serra Angel</p>
  </header>

  <!-- Main Content -->
  <main class="container">
    <article class="about-article">
      
      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          At the first Magic: The Gathering World Championship in 1994, Serra Angel was the win condition. 
          More than just a powerful card, she became an implicit reference for what it means to “control a game and play with righteousness.” 
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          The principle behind Zak Dolan's Angel Stasis deck—used by the first MTG World Champion—was to lock down the opponent's game, preventing them from untapping their lands and creatures. 
          Once the game was frozen, victory was secured inexorably through four copies of Serra Angel. 
          Zak Dolan understood that mastering tempo and resource management mattered more than simply playing the most powerful cards available. 
          Serra Angel thus asserted herself as a clear and deliberate win condition. 
          Patience, defense, and time management took precedence over surprise or excess.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          Her iconic ability—attacking without tapping, which would later become the static ability Vigilance—creates strong coherence between mechanics and symbolism. 
          The Angel acts without ever abandoning her protective role. She attacks without exposing herself, dominates without brutality. 
          This constant vigilance became, for many players, an expression of in-game righteousness: a victory achieved without trickery, without complexity, without sudden imbalance. 
          The card openly declares its power, allows time to respond, and conceals none of its intent. 
          Serra Angel is a simple card; I believe it is the only card to appear every year in regular sets since the game's inception. 
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          In 1996, Serra Angel was used on the Pro Tour in a Selesnya (white-green) deck by Bertrand Lestree. 
          The strategy involved summoning either Erhnam Djinn or Serra Angel and then, on the following turn, destroying all lands with Armageddon, hence the name of the archetype, ErhnamGeddon. 
          This is why you will find a reference to Armageddon in Michele Braschi's work. That same year, Eric Tam used Serra Angel in a Naya (white-red-green) deck. 
          Both decks contained only a single copy of Serra Angel, clearly showing that as early as 1996 the card was neither overpowered nor indispensable in competitive play. 
          Its power would continue to diminish over time, even though it was reprinted as recently as 2025 in the Foundations set
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          Rarely in the history of Magic has a card embodied such a precise alignment between illustration, symbolism, and mechanics. 
          A calm figure, an embodiment of power, a constant presence on the battlefield, Serra Angel became the symbol of a friendly, accessible Magic—a game played with simplicity and clarity.
        </p>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>

